WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:03.980
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:08.930
We'll start with a quick recap on Metropolis
Light Transport and then discuss a cool technique

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:10.300
that builds on top of it.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:14.620
If we would like to see how digitally modeled
objects would look like in real life, we would

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:20.019
create a 3D model of the desired scene, assign
material models to the objects within, and

00:00:20.019 --> 00:00:24.919
use a photorealistic rendering algorithm to
finish the job. It simulates rays of light

00:00:24.919 --> 00:00:29.470
that connect the camera to the light sources
in the scene and compute the flow of energy

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:30.669
between them.

00:00:30.669 --> 00:00:34.890
Initially, after a few rays, we'll only have
a rough idea on how the image should look

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:40.070
like, therefore our initial results will contain
a substantial amount of noise. We can get

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:45.360
rid of this by simulating the path of millions
and millions of rays that will eventually

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.810
clean up our image. This process, where a
noisy image gets clearer and clearer, we call

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:56.530
convergence, and the problem is that this
can take excruciatingly long, even up to hours

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:58.970
to get a perfectly clear image.

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:03.120
With the simpler algorithms out there, we
generate these light paths randomly. This

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.990
technique we call path tracing. However, in
the scene that you see here, most random paths

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:13.630
can't connect the camera and the light source
because this wall is in the way, obstructing

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:18.799
many of them. Light paths like these don't
contribute anything to our calculations and

00:01:18.799 --> 00:01:22.819
are ultimately a waste of time and resources.

00:01:22.819 --> 00:01:27.929
After generating hundreds of random light
paths, we have found a path that finally connects

00:01:27.929 --> 00:01:33.009
the camera with the light source without any
obstructions. When generating the next path,

00:01:33.009 --> 00:01:37.369
it would be a crime to not use this knowledge
to our advantage. A technique called

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:42.740
Metropolis Light Transport will make sure to use this
valuable knowledge, and upon finding a bright

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:48.359
light path, it will explore other paths that
are nearby, to have the best shot at creating

00:01:48.359 --> 00:01:50.999
valid, unobstructed connections.

00:01:50.999 --> 00:01:56.039
If we have a difficult scene at hand, Metropolis Light Transport gives us way better results

00:01:56.039 --> 00:02:00.670
than traditional, completely random path sampling
techniques, such as path tracing.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:06.340
This scene is extremely difficult in a sense
that the only source of light is coming from

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:11.060
the upper left, and after the light goes through
multiple glass spheres, most of the light

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:17.310
paths that we generate will be invalid. As
you can see, this is a valiant effort with

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:23.510
random path tracing that yields really dreadful
results. Metropolis Light Transport is extremely

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:27.790
useful in these cases and therefore should
always be the weapon of choice.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:32.910
However, it is more expensive to compute than
traditional random sampling. This means that

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:37.970
if we have an easy scene on our hands, this
smart Metropolis sampling doesn't pay off

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:42.380
and performs worse than a naive technique
in the same amount of time.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:48.680
So, on easy scenes, traditional random sampling,
difficult scenes, Metropolis sampling. Super

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:54.140
simple, super intuitive, but the million dollar
question is how to mathematically formulate

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.930
and measure what an easy and what a difficult
scene is.

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:02.730
This problem is considered extremely difficult
and was left open in the Metropolis Light Transport

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.170
paper in 2002.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:09.270
Even if we knew what to look for, we would
likely get an answer by creating a converged

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:14.900
image of the scene, which, without the knowledge
of what algorithm to use, may take up to days

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:20.620
to complete. But if we've created the image,
it's too late, so we would need this information

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:25.900
before we start this rendering process. This
way we can choose the right algorithm on the

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:27.260
first try.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:31.850
With this technique that came more than ten
years after the Metropolis paper, it is possible

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:37.400
to mathematically formalize and quickly decide
whether a scene is easy or difficult. The

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:42.290
key insight is that in a difficult scene we
often experience that a completely random

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:48.030
ray is very likely to be invalid. This insight,
with two other simple metrics gives us all

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:53.060
the knowledge we need to decide whether a
scene is easy or difficult, and the algorithm

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:58.380
tells us what mixture of the two sampling
techniques we exactly need to use to get beautiful

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.130
images quickly.

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:04.730
The more complex light transport algorithms
get, the more efficient they become, but at

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:10.580
the same time, we're wallowing in parameters
that we need to set up correctly to get adequate

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:15.880
results quickly. This way, we have an algorithm
that doesn't take any parameters. You just

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:21.160
fire it up, and forget about it. Like a good
employee, it knows when to work smart, and

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:26.790
when a dumb solution with a lot of firepower
is better. And, it was tested on a variety

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:30.160
of scenes and found close to optimal settings.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:34.520
Implementing this technique is remarkably
easy, someone who is familiar with the basics

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:37.470
of light transport can do it in less than
half an hour.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:41.060
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

